{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion","id":1120287,"title":"DE2: Arthurian discussion","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-25T03:40:21-07:00","id":1120287,"title":"DE2: Arthurian discussion","content":"If the Arthurian era gets selected in the next DE2 vote, how would you like to see it handled? I think you pretty much have to have Mage as one of the two gamelines for this era (because Acanthus symbology took on its current flavor in no small part due to a Camelot of The Time Before), though the Arthurian Era in the current iteration of reality should definitely <i>not</i> be the Legendary Arthur: more like the movie from a few years back, where Arthur was a Roman soldier who stayed behind when the Romans pulled out of Britain.<br /> <br /> For the other gameline, I'd probably go with Changeling. But that's just me; and I could also see Beast as the second gameline, subverting the \u201cknights killing monsters\u201d notion that's so prevalent in Arthurian-based stories. <br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T03:52:10-07:00","id":1120289,"title":"","content":"Don't forget Sir Marrock, the round table's resident werewolf","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T04:21:17-07:00","id":1120303,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion#post1120287\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Arthurian era gets selected in the next DE2 vote, how would you like to see it handled? I think you pretty much have to have Mage as one of the two gamelines for this era (because Acanthus symbology took on its current flavor in no small part due to a Camelot of The Time Before), though the Arthurian Era in the current iteration of reality should definitely <i>not</i> be the Legendary Arthur: more like the movie from a few years back, where Arthur was a Roman soldier who stayed behind when the Romans pulled out of Britain.<br /> <br /> For the other gameline, I'd probably go with Changeling. But that's just me; and I could also see Beast as the second gameline, subverting the \u201cknights killing monsters\u201d notion that's so prevalent in Arthurian-based stories. <br /> <br /> Thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd go Werewolf and Changeling. A single path's imagery hardly justifies inclusion in a Era IMO.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T04:34:55-07:00","id":1120307,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion#post1120287\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Arthurian era gets selected in the next DE2 vote, how would you like to see it handled? I think you pretty much have to have Mage as one of the two gamelines for this era (because Acanthus symbology took on its current flavor in no small part due to a Camelot of The Time Before), though the Arthurian Era in the current iteration of reality should definitely <i>not</i> be the Legendary Arthur: more like the movie from a few years back, where Arthur was a Roman soldier who stayed behind when the Romans pulled out of Britain.<br /> <br /> For the other gameline, I'd probably go with Changeling. But that's just me; and I could also see Beast as the second gameline, subverting the \u201cknights killing monsters\u201d notion that's so prevalent in Arthurian-based stories.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>I pretty much agree 100%. You can also rest happy knowing that Dave mentioned in one of his posts, that the earlier, grittier Arthur is exactly what he had in mind for the era, not the romantic Arthur. Then again, we are talking about the Ruins of the Time Before, that resulted from Merlin's Ascension and subsequent Exarch raging reality edits. Contradictory nature of these ruins allows for the writer of the era to actually describe multiple variant timelines of the story. This nicely aligns with the many real-world reinterpretations of the Arthurian mythos.<br /> <br /> So, the Lady of the Lake could be at the same time an apprentice of Merlin, True Fae, powerful Fairest Changeling, Supernal Being or perhaps one of the Legendary creatures that are supposed to be described in the Signs of Sorcery. The opportunities this kind of unique storytelling offers are almost a crime to pass up.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12191_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12191\" data-vbnamecard=\"12191\">Malus</a> The same goes for Werewolf imagery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5973-prometheus","name":"Prometheus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5973_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T05:09:02-07:00","id":1120315,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120307#post1120307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> The same goes for Werewolf imagery.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not just imagery, but two setting books drawing inspiration directly from said history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T05:27:10-07:00","id":1120320,"title":"","content":"So how would people see:<br /> Vampire,<br /> Promethean,<br /> Hunter,<br /> Geist,<br /> Mummy,<br /> Demon, or<br /> Deviant<br /> in this setting? (Instead of the low hanging fruit)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T07:08:45-07:00","id":1120343,"title":"","content":"Alright, so, this whole era is super, super relevant to my interests. For a while, my all-consuming geeky passion was Arthurian legend and the history that inspired it, that eventually expanded increasingly further backwards until I had a thing for ancient history period, but Fifth Century Post-Roman Britain is one of my favourites. So, here's how I understand this particularly messy historical era, which is so fun to write for because the history is mostly blank while myth and legend fills in the gaps.<br /> <br /> <b>The Thing With The Goths</b><br /> <br /> So, the Late Roman Empire was not nearly as martial as Principate Rome was. The ideal citizen was no longer a farmer who became a soldier, did their duty on behalf of the state, and then went home. The line between Citizen-Soldier and Auxiliary Mercenary blurred with the Antonine Decree which freely granted citizenship to all inhabitants of the Empire, more or less undercutting the main motivation to joining the legions in the first place. Rome eventually realized they couldn't conquer the world, so they set up a large network of enormous &quot;Screw Off&quot; border forts called the <i>limes</i> meant to keep out barbarians. These were supplemented with client kingdoms and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foederati\" target=\"_blank\">foederati</a> that more or less took the brunt of assaults from invaders trying to get away from successive waves of migratory people who were all fleeing the wave of death that was the Huns, and the &quot;paper&quot; strength of military units kept outstripping the actual numbers. Military forces became increasingly tied to regional government and regional authorities as opposed to a larger centralized government. Civic leaders became military leaders. One of the main <i>limes </i>was known as &quot;The Saxon Coast&quot; on the east side of Britain, and had its own Count (high-ranking civic/military official) with a force of 2,250 men. It's quite likely they had some Saxon groups in their employ.<br /> <br /> When the Goths revolted against Rome and moved to raze the Eternal City (that will happen when you short people you expect to die for you on their pay) some Roman troops withdrew, and the British were left with the impression that they were coming back. Spoilers! They weren't. And the remaining soldiers weren't getting paid, so the British had to figure out what in the hell they were going to do. Men appointed themselves Emperor of Britain and were quickly pulled down, but not without dragging more soldiers off to Gaul to die. That's when <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_III_(Western_Roman_Emperor)\" target=\"_blank\">Constantine III</a> did his thing. He declared himself an Emperor of the West (or may have been exalted as Emperor by his troops, who were among the Roman military units left in Britain) to which Honorius, the Emperor in Rome, more or less said &quot;sure, knock yourself out&quot; because Constantine III was threatening him to the north while <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaric_I\" target=\"_blank\">Alaric</a> was rampaging unchecked in Italy. Constantine III took the majority of the troops that were left with him to the continent, and eventually was made Emperor of Britain, Gaul, and Spain. This didn't last. The invading tribes crossed the Pyrenees, and at the same time Saxon and Irish pirates started raiding Britain. It's entirely possible some of the Saxons were unpaid foederati who get very fed up of being stuck on the far eastern shore. So Spain revolted under a new Emperor, Britain and the territory that became Brittany withdrew because screw the guy that left them undefended to play at being king, and Constantine III was captured and executed.<br /> <br /> <b>The Thing With The Bishops</b><br /> <br /> After Constantine III went down, the British kind of had to fend for themselves. Important landowners ended up becoming petty kings of territories that started forming along old British tribal lands, and things got messy. There was a brief respite at some point around 410 CE when the British gave up on Constantine III and figured out what they were going to do, and the landowners/petty kings set themselves up as a council that was probably structured at least somewhat after the Roman government. They probably identified more as Roman landowners than kings at this point. The guy they put in charge may have been this fellow named Vitalinus, who was important in London and may have been a Bishop but was also a big shot around the area that would become Gloucester, and eventually became King of Powys. He was married to a descendent of Constantine the Great, and the daughter of another British pretender Emperor, Magnus Flavius Maximum. You would probably know him better as <a href=\"http://www.vortigernstudies.org.uk/vortigernhomepage.htm\" target=\"_blank\">Vortigern</a>. Yeah. The tyrannical king of Arthurian legend was probably an <i>arch</i><i>bishop </i>and might have even been recognized as a <i>saint</i>. So after establishing a functioning British government, Vitalinus/Vortigern decided to try and hold off the invaders with what Roman military might they had left. They got very lucky ~430 CE because the fight was <i>not </i>going well, but <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanus_of_Auxerre\" target=\"_blank\">Bishop (later Saint) Germanus of Auxerre,</a> a retired Dux and lawyer, happened to be in Britain to confront <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelagianism\" target=\"_blank\">Pelagianism</a>. According to the accounts that have survived, Pelagianism was popular with the wealthy land owners and with the British in general, and though Germanus' position was unpopular he won his debates by sheer rhetorical skill and force of personality. It's entirely possible that Germanus' primary debate opponent was Vortigern himself.<br /> <br /> And then an invasion happened. Again.<br /> <br /> Germanus managed to lead the British troops to victory by using the terrain and deception to give the impression they had a significantly larger army than they actually did, and the enemy (traditionally Saxon but possibly Gael) fled. Some have argued that combatting Pelgianism, which had a focus on personal responsibility and good works, may have undercut British morale. Germanus may have visited again in the early 440s, where legend has it Vortigern tried to pin his daughter's illegitimate child on the Bishop as a form of blackmail. Germanus, however, saved the asses of the British again, intervening when General <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavius_Aetius\" target=\"_blank\">Flavius Aetius</a>, a childhood friend of Atilla the Hun and a man so awesome that he is one of a few given the title The Last Roman, was planning to invade Brittany to punish it for seceding from the Western Empire. He may have also delivered a letter to Aetius that is famously called &quot;The Groans of the British&quot; where the British council of landowners/kings begged for military support from Rome because they couldn't keep doing this. No help was sent. The reason? Oh, you know, Atilla the Hun. Scourge of God. Also the Emperor might still be a little sore about the Constantine III thing. So, what are the British to do? Vortigern famously (disastrously) decided to do what the Romans did- hire barbarians to fight barbarians. Enter Hengst and Horsa, two brothers who may or may not be legendary culture heroes instead of historical figures (but that's true of a decent amount of people in this time period), and the Jutes, who Vortigern settled in such numbers in Kent that to people writing at the time in Gaul it looked like a full-blown occupation of the country. Hengst and Horsa <i>did </i>do a good job of solving the Gaelic problem though.<br /> <br /> Except now there's a Jute problem.<br /> <br /> Oh, that's right, the main reason they're in this mess in the first place? The fact that the leaders of Britannia can't pay to field large-scale semi-permanent military forces? It's still a problem. Vortigern tries to hold off this problem. He cedes more land to the Jutes. He divorces his wife (which probably destroyed his support with his Romano-British power base) and marries Hengst's daughter. None of it works, because they promised a hell of a lot more than they can give and the Jutes don't give a damn about stockpiles of Roman currency that are next to useless. Eventually, there's a Jute revolt, and there's a simultaneous Romano-British revolt, and Vortigern's regime goes down.<br /> <br /> But that's a story for another post.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 07-25-2017, 10:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T08:29:44-07:00","id":1120359,"title":"","content":"Malus - it's not one Path's symbology. The Arthurian connection is in (deep breath)<br /> <br /> Mage: The Awakening first edition corebook<br /> Tome of the Watchtowers<br /> Tome of the Mysteries<br /> Silver Ladder<br /> Astral Realms<br /> Seers of the Throne<br /> Imperial Mysteries<br /> Left Hand Path<br /> Mage: The Awakening second edition<br /> Signs of Sorcery<br /> Dancers in the Dusk (bonus changeling book!)<br /> <br /> It's got considerably more publishing history behind it than werewolf's connection to Babylon, and you don't see anyone denying *that*.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":12}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T08:57:14-07:00","id":1120368,"title":"","content":"Just based on its connections, it seems like if an Arthurian era won it would be 'locked in' with Mage and Changeling, with Nimue being thought to be Fae and Merlin and Mordred being mages, not just because of Dancers in the Dark. I can't imagine an Arthur era focusing on Sir Marrock next to that whole thing, and hunting doesn't seem to be a big deal. A cynical side of me wants to note that it's entirely possible the era will go Promethean and Beast. A centimani doesn't have enough fingers to count how many times I've been wrong. Plus since the Sundering and the lack of Pangaeans there's less keeping Uratha and Awakened at a similar level.<br /> <br /> I guess you could associate Mordred's side of things with the Pure. I've never been clear on how Aeons work, but apparently before there was Mordred there was Set? The Wiki suggests that, but Imperial Mysteries says it was the Furies.<br /> <br /> Into other gamelines, keeping in mind my limited knowledge of Arthurian myth. In more than a couple depictions I've seen the forces that brought Camelot down depicted as vampire-like entities. Though the Sheeda could be described as vampire-fae. Alternatively you could have Arthur as the vampire, or Merlin. The Knights are blood bound, and it's either an attempt to make an earlier-era Sun-Walking Knights, the covenant with vampires and ghouls on equal footing, or a proto-Invictus stunt to get a massive and loyal herd.<br /> <br /> Werewolf I mentioned, Pure from Mordred's point of view. Literally any aspect of the story, from the round table to the sword to Arthur, Merlin and so on, could be spirits and followed by lodges or a pack. The size of the Knights of the Round Table tilts towards the larger end, more appropriate for the Pure than Forsaken. Possible Ivory Claws?<br /> <br /> Promethean was fun. Somewhere you have a lineage being born. Either Arthur or the Knights are some newfangled Promethean who attempts to embody the highest virtues of humanity, eschewing the lower. Or they have some Disquiet that acts counter to usual.<br /> <br /> Hunter I had the hardest time with. I mean, they're knights so I guess they fight monsters, but I don't know of any in particular. This would be hugely unpopular but one of Cheiron's backgrounds has them as a vanguard of invaders from a world where the Vigil failed. Could be that after centuries of working on the fringes of things they decided to put an outpost on Earth in the form of Camelot. The Round Table is the Board.<br /> <br /> Geist. Not my favorite game, but I thought it would be funny if Arthur was a Sin-Eater and Merlin was his geist. Because what would be worse than a ghost demanding you do what they want? A ghost mage demanding you forge a new Atlantis.<br /> <br /> Mummy is similar, with some sort of attempt to revitalize Irem or, alternatively, just use it as a base of operations for relic-retrieval. Sort of like Archaic.<br /> <br /> Demon is like Mummy, with an attempt to find Hell or at least capitalize upon it.<br /> <br /> Beast I've a hard time with. I could see it as two warring factions, but Arthur doesn't seem to represent any fearful lessons. I suppose after the Roman occupation you would have natives who did not want to see a ruler rise again. Maybe Arthur as Hero?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 07-25-2017, 01:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T10:28:01-07:00","id":1120397,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I really like this setting for Mage and Changeling. Other games can be cool, but these two would work perfectly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/196-max-deltree","name":"Max Deltree","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"178"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T10:35:34-07:00","id":1120399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120359#post1120359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Malus - it's not one Path's symbology. The Arthurian connection is in (deep breath)<br /> <br /> Mage: The Awakening first edition corebook<br /> Tome of the Watchtowers<br /> Tome of the Mysteries<br /> Silver Ladder<br /> Astral Realms<br /> Seers of the Throne<br /> Imperial Mysteries<br /> Left Hand Path<br /> Mage: The Awakening second edition<br /> Signs of Sorcery<br /> Dancers in the Dusk (bonus changeling book!)<br /> <br /> It's got considerably more publishing history behind it than werewolf's connection to Babylon, and you don't see anyone denying *that*.</div> </div> </div> </div>How so? What's the common thread there beyond the archetype that Merlin represents?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T10:38:22-07:00","id":1120402,"title":"","content":"Oh geez, I've got a head of steam now, all pumped up because this geek train does not have brakes! (Also, holy crap, where was I storing all this information when I wasn't using it? It's just... pouring on out of me).<br /> <br /> <b>Fifth Century Britain Part 2: Saxon Boogaloo</b><br /> <br /> So the 440s are right when actual historical records in Britain fall to pieces, and the timeline gets extremely fuzzy. Things we know for sure: Vortigern had a decent amount of children. His sons by Sevira, daughter of Magnus Maximum were, in order, Vortimer, Catigern, Pascent (these two are often switched around), and Faustus/Britu (this guy is sometimes considered his grandson, and sometimes as two separate sons). He also had a daughter, Scotnoe, who married a prince of Ireland. Seems Vortigern had a thing for using marriage to try and broker peace. Vortimer, much like Vortigern, was an assumed name chosen for political reasons (both roughly mean &quot;High King&quot;, but Vortimer has the stronger emphasis on that, one-upping his dad). Vortimer's actually name <i>may </i>have been Theodosius or Teithfallt, and there's strong indication to suggest that Vortimer was living as a monk/minor military leader in Brittany at the time, and may have returned <i>with </i>Germanus to confront his father. Vortigern had a son with Rowena, daughter or sister of Hengst, Aesc, who became the first proper king of Kent.<br /> <br /> Why was employing Hengst and Horsa such a bad idea? Well, every source written about them mentions they were exiles from the homeland, which for people of their standing meant they must have done something fairly bad (they even get a mention in Beowulf). So there's your first red flag. Secondly, they kept inviting increasingly large numbers of ships from Jutland to settle in Kent, quickly outgrowing the stipend granted to them by Vortigern, which no one really made an effort to stop because more warriors meant more ability to fend off the Gaels and the Picts. But even the Saxon sources admit that Hengst was probably planning on betraying him the entire time, and only used the major resource difficulties as an excuse (though it was probably a pretty genuine motivation for the nobles and commoners under him). For simplicity reasons, I'm going to relay the timeline that is most readily accepted and reasonable with the least Romance era stuff.<ul><li>The Jutes betray the Britons for the first time after Vortigern's disgrace in the early 440s by Germanus. At this time, Catigern and Pascent are co-regents of Powys (north-eastern Wales) and Gwent (south-eastern Wales) is waiting for Vortimer/Theodosius' return. Vortigern is in London negotiating with Hengst and Horsa and the British Council.<ul><li>The disgrace of Vortigern may have happened <i>at </i>the negotiations and the betrayal of Hengst and Horsa came in the wake of Vortimer's taking control of the closest thing to a High Kingship the British have at the time.</li> <li>Germanus' return to Gaul and subsequent delivering of messages from the British Council to Aetius marks the last official communication between Britain and Rome. Everything goes dark and fuzzy from here on out.</li> </ul></li> <li>The British have already been refortifying old Celtic hill forts to defend against the Irish, and have been fortifying Roman settlements above and beyond their normal defences. The Southern and Western British Kingdoms establish a solid base of operations and last line of defence in the style of Roman <i>limes </i>along the Welsh border. Central Britain is, technically, still British but it is conflicted territory between Briton and Anglo-Saxon.</li> <li>Between the 440s and ~455-456 CE Vortimer leads the British against the Saxons, who keep encroaching closer and closer to the British's Point of No Return. If Saxon forces take the mouth of the Severn River, it's game over. The British kingdoms will be divided into four more easily managed chunks; Wales, Cornwall, South Britain, and North Britain.<ul><li>As far as we know, Vortigern is AWOL. He might be a Saxon hostage at this point, or may have just returned to being archbishop of London. Some legends say that he retreated to Wales to rule as a minor king and later died in a fire when his fortress was struck by lightning.</li> <li>After several battles over the years, there is something of a mutual loss between the forces of Vortimer and Hengst at Avebury. Each leader loses a brother, Vortimer loses Catigern and Hengst loses Horsa. The two leaders agree to retire to nearby Stonehenge as neutral ground and negotiate a truce.</li> </ul></li> <li>The negotiation at Stonehenge is later referred to in legend as the Night of the Long Knives. A heck of a lot of the British leaders who attended in good faith were butchered, and Vortimer either dies in the fighting or is poisoned by Rowena, his step-mother.<ul><li>In the wake of this, Gwent goes to Vortimer's daughter Modron who marries Honorius of Gwent (also called Ynyr Gwent), and their son or grandson is Caradoc Long-Arm, a warrior associated with Arthur in later legend. Pascent quietly takes up the kingship of (at least part of) Powys.</li> </ul></li> </ul><b>What Have the Romans Ever Done For Us?</b><br /> <br /> So, Vortigern himself, as a Pelagian archbishop and leader of the British Council is theorized to be part of some kind of nationalist party that favoured the return of the tribal kingdoms and British independence, but there is still a not insignificant faction among the British (particularly the Southern British landowners around what would later become Devon and Cornwall) that still want contact with Rome. These were people whose livelihood, for the most part, depended on contact with the outside world. In the meantime, while all the Gaelic and Pictish raiding was going on, Brittany had acquired the reputation of harbouring outlaws of the Roman Empire (which is why Aetius was off to teach them a lesson) and they had a standing alliance with the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Soissons\" target=\"_blank\">Kingdom of Soissons</a>. During Vortigern's rise to power, and especially during the stormy 420s, amongst his woes weren't just the Gaels and the Picts, &quot;but especially the dread of Ambrosius&quot;. This is usually interpreted to mean Ambrosius Aurelianus, badass Romano-British leader and declared in legend the uncle of Arthur (historically he might have <i>been </i>Arthur), but this seems unlikely because then Ambrosius must have been stupidly old when he took the fight to the Saxons in the 460s if he was a contemporary of Vortigern. <br /> <br /> It is more likely that there were <i>two </i>individuals named Ambrosius, father and son. The only contemporary writer in Britain at the time (St. Gildas, writing from the 6th Century) says that Ambrosius Aurelianus' family had &quot;worn the purple&quot; which meant they were at least military tribunes, and possibly as high-ranking as consuls. What individual could have wielded such political authority that Vortigern was more frightened of him than the invaders? Some scholars suggest that Ambrosius the Elder was actually the last Roman governor of Maxima Caesariensis, who arrived from Gaul in the 410s and helped establish the British Council under the orders of Emperor Honorius. <br /> <br /> It is hypothesized that the ceding of Kent (a neighbouring territory) to Hengst and Horsa was the result of the Roman faction led by Ambrosius the Elder (who provided most of the provisions for the Saxons from their territories) refusing to increase the provisions they would give up to the Saxons, which at least in part provided the motivation for Hengst to later betray Vortigern. This ultimately resulted in a battle around 437 CE between Ambrosius' faction and Vortigern's at Wallop. It is likely that Ambrosius the Elder didn't die here, as Gildas says that Ambrosius Aurelianus was The Last Roman, whose family had been slain &quot;in these same broils&quot; which suggests Ambrosius the Elder died in the 440s or, at the absolute latest, at the Night of the Long Knives. Ambrosius the Younger had earlier, likely in the 440s, been sent away to a Roman-sympathizing part of the country that wasn't anywhere near his family's estates, where he was found playing with some friends by Vortigern or one of his relatives. Legend has it they were planning to kill him, but they changed their minds, perhaps fearing retaliation, perhaps just due to a change of heart.<br /> <br /> Up until the late 450s, Ambrosius the Younger has mostly stayed out of the political scene. Here's what likely happened next:<ul><li>In the wake of the Night of the Long Knives, Hengst and his forces go home. A not insignificant portion of Eastern Britain is now Saxon territory.</li> <li>Ambrosius the Younger explodes onto the political/military scene. He establishes an entrenched British resistance building off of Vortimer's efforts, making the re-established fortifications into fixed, garrisoned defensive strongholds, with well-maintained patrol routes to isolate pockets of Anglo-Saxon settlement, and formed a highly-mobile, quick reaction, putative raiding force. This regrouping of the British occurred between the mid-450s and the mid-460s.<ul><li>Following the regrouping and the relative peace, wherein the Saxons took control of pretty large swathes of central Britain, Ambrosius goes on the offensive in a blistering assault that completely reverses the invasions of the last few decades. By the time someone is writing a biography of Germanus in the late 470s Britain is described as a prosperous place, and trade from as far as the Byzantine empire is reaching places like Tintagel Castle.</li> </ul></li> <li>Ambrosius the Younger is the de facto, and then official, High King of the British (at least in Central/Southern Britain and Wales) from the 460s until his death.<ul><li>During this time period, Brittany becomes increasingly known as a safe haven for former Roman citizens with no lands to worry about in the wake of the Visigoth invaders currently hacking apart what was left of Roman Gaul. It helps that the Kingdom of Soissons acted as a buffer state.</li> </ul></li> <li>A message from Roman Gaul makes it to Britain, begging for aid from a British ruler called &quot;Riothamus&quot; (likely a title as it means Highest King). It is likely that Riothamus was Ambrosius the Younger. Ambrosius moves from war in Britain to war in Gaul, possibly entering the war with the Visigoths in 468 or in 470.<ul><li>There is a letter from a Gallic bishop, Sidonius Apollinaris to Riothamus addressing him as &quot;my friend&quot;, and the surviving letter reads in a very frustrated manner. It seems that the charismatic (and likely highly opportunistic) hard-hitting British raiders had something of a reputation of luring slaves and servants away from land owners and off to a life of adventure.</li> <li>If Riothamus/Ambrosius entered the war in 468, it is very likely they were betrayed by the Praetorian Prefect of Gaul, Arvandus, who was communicating with King Euric of the Visigoths, betraying the British position and suggesting that the Visigoths and the Burgundians (then a Roman client state) should divide Gaul between them. Arvandus was arrested and later executed, but it was too late. Euric and the Visigoths ambushed and slaughtered the British forces under Riothamus (supposedly around 12,000 men) before Roman reinforcements could arrive. Riothamus survived the battle and fled to the Burgundians, who had <i>not </i>betrayed Rome. Roman record never mentions Riothamus again.</li> </ul></li> </ul>Either way, if Riothamus is Ambrosius or somebody else from Britain we don't know about, that is still a lot of soldiers to lose in a single conflict. Ambrosius/Riothamus likely returned to Britain in the mid-470s, but disrupted communications and rumour likely preceded him, and by the 480s the Saxons were confident that Ambrosius was dead and were starting to tentatively (and then with increasing boldness) go on the offensive again. <br /> <br /> Sadly for them, Ambrosius was not dead.<br /> <br /> Sadly for Ambrosius, he was back to where he started almost twenty years ago, with fewer soldiers than before.<br /> <br /> This is when a particularly famous series of twelve battles comes into play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T10:49:46-07:00","id":1120409,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120399#post1120399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How so? What's the common thread there beyond the archetype that Merlin represents?</div> </div> </div> </div>Specifically Merlin, who was less of an archetype and more of an actual dude. Also in Imperial Mysteries and Astral Realms we see Mordred and Gloriana, the Faerie Queen, as Aeons.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T12:11:00-07:00","id":1120442,"title":"","content":"An actual dude whose creations litter the UK in the CofD, and who founded a Legacy that (Dresden-like) still calls their leader &quot;Merlin&quot; as a title. The current holder of which is one of Awakening's original signature characters.<br /> <br /> EDIT:This isn't like saying &quot;I think the Babylonian era is more suited to werewolves and vampires&quot;, this is like saying &quot;we should have a Bram Stoker era and make Dracula a Beast, ignoring the Ordo Dracul and his brides already existing as Requiem characters.&quot;<br /> <br /> ALSO EDIT: And lest you think I'm arguing out of self-interest: I voted for Jazz Age.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":14,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","user":"Dave Brookshaw","date":". 07-25-2017, 03:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":12}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T13:03:02-07:00","id":1120454,"title":"","content":"I voted for Jazz Age and Werewolf is my favorite line. I also voted Geist for Golden Age of Piracy. My reasoning is that Forsaken by Rome (which is a kickass setting!) has a bit of a similar vibe with what Arthurian Werewolf might accomplish. And I really wanna see a full on Arthurian Mage setting, with Changeling crossover. It's just a perfect fit!<br /> <br /> I prefer Werewolf for the babylonian setting. Mix if with the Werewolves of Bau and it would kick ass (plus, Beast naming and all that).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/196-max-deltree","name":"Max Deltree","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"178"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T13:20:28-07:00","id":1120464,"title":"","content":"I see Arthurian Britain as a conflict between the Diamond/Seers from Rome with the native Nameless Orders, with Merlin being the heart of an attempt to unite the Diamond and Nameless without slaughtering one or the other. It would be a conflict between the powers behind the thrones, instead of focusing upon the rulers themselves.<br /> \u200bFor Changelings Camelot would be an attempt to defend against the Keepers, using iron weapons to push the True Fae back into their dark corners.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T13:28:19-07:00","id":1120468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120442#post1120442\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">An actual dude whose creations litter the UK in the CofD, and who founded a Legacy that (Dresden-like) still calls their leader &quot;Merlin&quot; as a title. The current holder of which is one of Awakening's original signature characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which Legacy is this? I only know a few. Though it would be Walkers in the Mist but they don't mention the Merlin thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> EDIT:This isn't like saying &quot;I think the Babylonian era is more suited to werewolves and vampires&quot;, this is like saying &quot;we should have a Bram Stoker era and make Dracula a Beast, ignoring the Ordo Dracul and his brides already existing as Requiem characters.&quot; </div> </div> </div>I agree it fits with Mage, Vent just asked for some other perspectives.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ALSO EDIT: And lest you think I'm arguing out of self-interest: I voted for Jazz Age. </div> </div> </div>I'm surprised. For Mage or what?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 07-25-2017, 04:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T14:05:09-07:00","id":1120481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120307#post1120307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I pretty much agree 100%. You can also rest happy knowing that Dave mentioned in one of his posts, that the earlier, grittier Arthur is exactly what he had in mind for the era, not the romantic Arthur. Then again, we are talking about the Ruins of the Time Before, that resulted from Merlin's Ascension and subsequent Exarch raging reality edits. Contradictory nature of these ruins allows for the writer of the era to actually describe multiple variant timelines of the story. This nicely aligns with the many real-world reinterpretations of the Arthurian mythos.<br /> <br /> So, the Lady of the Lake could be at the same time an apprentice of Merlin, True Fae, powerful Fairest Changeling, Supernal Being or perhaps one of the Legendary creatures that are supposed to be described in the Signs of Sorcery. The opportunities this kind of unique storytelling offers are almost a crime to pass up.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12191_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12191\" data-vbnamecard=\"12191\">Malus</a> The same goes for Werewolf imagery.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I agree, the opportunities for multiple, contradictory stories is superb and not to be passed up. My take on the period and Merlin's ascension play, with it's lingering impact on literature, chivalry and romance, is that the Dark Era <i><b>needs</b></i> to capture the element of--for lack of a better word--heroic glory that he generated in the play. Why? This is a world of Darkness! Rar!<br /> <br /> Well yes, but without light you don't have darkness. People sat around in the Middle Ages writing and retelling Arthurian stories as a way of highlighting human nature, what chivalry was supposed to be, and the bright possibility for mankind. It also, thereby, pointed out how things were crap, in comparison. It's valuable in a storytelling game to show that light being generated, played in, and then snuffed out. It creates context. That the bright, soaring golden age is wrought by the blatant and intrusive enchantment of the supernatural just means that later on everyone gets to point to the flaming wreckage and say &quot;do not do that.&quot; <br /> <br /> Still, the flight's worth it.<br /> <br /> Or maybe it's not? Maybe it really all is a lie, and Arthur was just a trumped-up marshal under High King Ambrosius Aurelianus who bought the British an extra generation by whipping the Saxons at Badon Hill? Or a warlord in the North who was momentarily successful before the Saxons obliterated the kingdom of his liege?<br /> <br /> I find these options less interesting, less mystical and less fulfilling than the fantastical alternative--especially since we know the Exarchs are going to crush Merlin's work to forgotten dust after he's gone, anyway. Go wild!<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T14:21:33-07:00","id":1120483,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120343#post1120343\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alright, so, this whole era is super, super relevant to my interests. For a while, my all-consuming geeky passion was Arthurian legend and the history that inspired it, that eventually expanded increasingly further backwards until I had a thing for ancient history period, but Fifth Century Post-Roman Britain is one of my favourites. So, here's how I understand this particularly messy historical era, which is so fun to write for because the history is mostly blank while myth and legend fills in the gaps.<br /> <br /> &lt;super big snip!&gt;</div> </div> </div> </div>Well done and certainly sufficient as an overview. But! The more you dig the more weird it gets, so if anyone has an interest feel free to dig more--this is the cliffnotes version. There may have been multiple Germanic uprisings in Britain, of which Hengest and crew were the second. There's Vortigern's son &quot;Vortimer&quot;--if he existed. Arguments over which land The Vortigern gave to Hengest. Who this Ambrosius Aurelianus fellow was; and whether he was the son of an earlier Aurelius Ambrosius.<br /> <br /> Then things <i><b>really</b></i> get complicated. <br /> <br /> Ok, so to splats? I think you pretty much have to include Mage, if you're going to cover the period of Merlin. After that it depends on whether you want to highlight the &quot;Fall of the Romano-British and Their Desperate Defense Against the Germanics!&quot; Or, &quot;A Bright Shining Camelot on a Hill&quot;<br /> <br /> If they pick 400-500 the period will most likely be the former. If they run it 450 to 550ish, then the later. <br /> <br /> If the later, then Werewolf would be best--though I'd dearly like to see the vampires handled. If the later it gets harder, as Changeling and/or Beast call out for treatment. I could see a Mage/Beast era being done, effectively approaching the legend from both sides.<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T14:32:26-07:00","id":1120489,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120442#post1120442\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">An actual dude whose creations litter the UK in the CofD, and who founded a Legacy that (Dresden-like) still calls their leader &quot;Merlin&quot; as a title. The current holder of which is one of Awakening's original signature characters.<br /> <br /> EDIT:This isn't like saying &quot;I think the Babylonian era is more suited to werewolves and vampires&quot;, this is like saying &quot;we should have a Bram Stoker era and make Dracula a Beast, ignoring the Ordo Dracul and his brides already existing as Requiem characters.&quot;<br /> <br /> ALSO EDIT: And lest you think I'm arguing out of self-interest: I voted for Jazz Age.</div> </div> </div> </div>And I voted Gilgamesh, for obvious reasons. Still seems like the Mage setting there would be &quot;Merlin's legacy&quot; (lack of capitalization on purpose).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T14:32:31-07:00","id":1120490,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120402#post1120402\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh geez, I've got a head of steam now, all pumped up because this geek train does not have brakes! (Also, holy crap, where was I storing all this information when I wasn't using it? It's just... pouring on out of me).<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>You cannot contain the Arthurian history meme. It lives, itself, and demands it be replicated....<br /> <br /> &lt;3<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T19:42:08-07:00","id":1120593,"title":"","content":"Even if the Arthurian setting doesn't win, this thread has been worth it for Arcane's info dumps alone. That said, I want it to win.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T19:59:04-07:00","id":1120600,"title":"","content":"I've said for years that I'd like to do a Arthurian version of Mage one day, with Avalon as Atlantis. Magical island which has disappeared into myth? Very yes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#800000\">Ian A. A. Watson</span></b><span style=\"color:#bdc3c7\">(he/she)</span><br /> <b>Onyx Path Community Manager<br /> Trinity Continuum Content Lead</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4-ianwatson","name":"IanWatson","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4_2.png","title":"Community Manager","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1512"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T21:04:46-07:00","id":1120626,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khanwulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120483#post1120483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well done and certainly sufficient as an overview. But! The more you dig the more weird it gets, so if anyone has an interest feel free to dig more--this is the cliffnotes version. There may have been multiple Germanic uprisings in Britain, of which Hengest and crew were the second. There's Vortigern's son &quot;Vortimer&quot;--if he existed. Arguments over which land The Vortigern gave to Hengest. Who this Ambrosius Aurelianus fellow was; and whether he was the son of an earlier Aurelius Ambrosius.<br /> <br /> Then things <i><b>really</b></i> get complicated.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah, I basically just condensed several years of my personal study, of reading the works of scholars and authors picking favoured narratives, and <i>other</i> scholars and authors shrugging and going &quot;Hell if I know. Were they real? Are <i>we </i>real? Who even knows man.&quot; This is just the least contentious version of the (possible) history which includes as many of the recognizable players as is realistic. I'm going to be focusing, for the most part, on the versions I, personally, find the most likely. If only because, even taken in bits and pieces, this is already one <i>hell </i>of a story without any magic getting involved.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120593#post1120593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even if the Arthurian setting doesn't win, this thread has been worth it for Arcane's info dumps alone. That said, I want it to win.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks Dataweaver! But I'm not quite done yet. We've still got to get through the ultimate Shrug of History- who the hell was Arthur, and did they even exist? With the one light spot of &quot;We know Badon happened, we just aren't sure exactly when or where.&quot; The lack of written records is incredibly frustrating, and Gildas' lack of mentioning an Arthur at all is perplexing, because in terms of naming conventions for people, places, and things, it goes like this.<br /> <br /> Britain before the 480s-520s: Practically nobody named anything even remotely close to Arthur. No places named after Arthur. Nobody gives a crap.<br /> <br /> Britain <i>after </i>the 520s: Arthurs! Arthurs everywhere! Prince Arthurs! King Arthurs! Arthur was at that hill! Arthur lived in this fortress! I heard Arthur killed a man here! I heard Arthur killed a giant there! This is Camlann! No, <i>this </i>is Camlann! Look, we've got his <i>bones</i>!<br /> <br /> The first, the <i>very first </i>mention of Arthur that even <i>suggests </i>the earliest of the legends is around 590, where a warrior in a futile last stand against Anglo-Saxon besiegers is said to be the kind of battlefield horror that gluts the ravens... but he wasn't Arthur. Somehow this guy lives and dies without leaving a shred of surviving material or literary evidence, leaves no sign of his passage, and somehow managed to catapult from Memetic Badass to Eternal Legend within several decades of his death. And we have <i>no idea </i>about what really happened.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 07-26-2017, 12:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T21:06:15-07:00","id":1120627,"title":"","content":"Has anything been said of the Grail in CofD?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T21:19:04-07:00","id":1120632,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120627#post1120627\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Has anything been said of the Grail in CofD?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there's a Relic in the Mummy corebook that was suggested to have inspired it.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Yup. The Maser Grail. And only a three-dot Regium too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 07-26-2017, 12:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T21:24:53-07:00","id":1120635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120632#post1120632\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think there's a Relic in the Mummy corebook that was suggested to have inspired it.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Yup. The Maser Grail. And only a three-dot Regium too.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course the Arisen would look at the fucking Grail and go &quot;yeah, that's a three-dotter at most.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T21:28:42-07:00","id":1120640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120627#post1120627\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Has anything been said of the Grail in CofD?</div> </div> </div> </div>The Bron bloodline of vampires seek it, as it's tied with their curse. They have different ideas of what it represents, however.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The main point of division among members of the bloodline is the very nature of the thing they seek: the Holy Grail itself. One camp, aligned with the Lancea Sanctum, states unequivocally that the bloodline\u2019s founder was cursed for sipping from the Cup of Christ, the Holy Grail, as it has been portrayed for nearly a thousand years. Another group, tied strongly to the Circle of the Crone, insists just as adamantly that the curse laid upon their line is far older than the kine\u2019s Messiah, and that the \u201cGrail\u201d was actually one of the magical cauldrons of Welsh and Celtic myth, which had the power to restore the dead to life and is believed to be an inspiration for the earliest Christianized Grail stories and a decidedly preclassical Kindred creation myth. Still other members of the bloodline espouse more radical theories, claiming that the Grail is actually a metaphor for an enchanted stone that fell from the heavens or even the mortal bloodline of Christ. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-25T21:53:20-07:00","id":1120654,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120635#post1120635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Of course the Arisen would look at the fucking Grail and go &amp;quot;yeah, that's a three-dotter at most.&amp;quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>If I get time tomorrow, I'm migrating this into the best quotes thread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T06:42:30-07:00","id":1120774,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120626#post1120626\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Britain <i>after </i>the 520s: Arthurs! Arthurs everywhere! Prince Arthurs! King Arthurs! Arthur was at that hill! Arthur lived in this fortress! I heard Arthur killed a man here! I heard Arthur killed a giant there! This is Camlann! No, <i>this </i>is Camlann! Look, we've got his <i>bones</i>!<br /> <br /> The first, the <i>very first </i>mention of Arthur that even <i>suggests </i>the earliest of the legends is around 590, where a warrior in a futile last stand against Anglo-Saxon besiegers is said to be the kind of battlefield horror that gluts the ravens... but he wasn't Arthur. Somehow this guy lives and dies without leaving a shred of surviving material or literary evidence, leaves no sign of his passage, and somehow managed to catapult from Memetic Badass to Eternal Legend within several decades of his death. And we have <i>no idea </i>about what really happened.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wait, so till 590s we do not have anything resembling Romance Arthur? Maybe it's like Merlin, as Silver Ladder Acanthus gone to one Roman commander in 450-480s and said to him: 'Hey, let's make this great kingdom, with your armies and my magic' - and then he let Arthur be killed and kingdom destroyed so he would Ascended? And then, as story become Supernal Truth, it REPEATED itself at least dozen times, with each new commander adding 'I must build my own kingdom!' And with passing years there is added this whole Roman Diamond 'we are Dragons' thing - and we end with 'Arthur is Pendragon, born/blessed by dragons' in end of early Middle Ages.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 07-26-2017, 12:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T06:45:58-07:00","id":1120776,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120626#post1120626\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm going to be focusing, for the most part, on the versions I, personally, find the most likely. If only because, even taken in bits and pieces, this is already one <i>hell </i>of a story without any magic getting involved.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can go weird without adding a bit of magic. For example: a Hengest shows up independently as a key character in the story of the Battle of Finnsburgh, set shortly before a Hengest shows up on British shores. In that battle Hengest essentially betrays the binding obligations of hospitality in order to avenge the death of his liege. It's a super-messy bit and could explain why he called himself an &quot;exile&quot; from Juteland (which was dominated by Danes, anyway, at that point) later.<br /> <br /> Yet, Hengest is not the progenitor of the Saxon line of kings. He doesn't show up in it at all, which at least means that whomever took over later either considered him of no import or was a hostile dynasty!<br /> <br /> 5th-6th century British history is a knife-fight in a (London) phone booth.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120626#post1120626\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who the hell was Arthur, and did they even exist? The lack of written records is incredibly frustrating, and Gildas' lack of mentioning an Arthur at all is perplexing...</div> </div> </div> </div>The speculation seems to be that Arthur (assuming it was a name and not a title, or nickname) managed to honk off enough people, especially in and around the Church, that they put no effort into promoting him. Gildas, for example, is supposed to have had a brother who Arthur killed. He wasn't about to give Arthur press in his sermon, and may have either edited him out or made a backhanded reference to Arthur by referring to the descendants of Ambrosius as being debased from their illustrious ancestor.<br /> <br /> Of course, we know little about this Ambrosius guy, too. So there's that.<br /> <br /> Best we can say is that folks were really busy doing important stuff and didn't slow down to write it out for us. Civilizational crisis does that....<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T07:54:46-07:00","id":1120796,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120593#post1120593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even if the Arthurian setting doesn't win, this thread has been worth it for Arcane's info dumps alone. That said, I want it to win.</div> </div> </div> </div>Arcanist. I'm certainly not that cool.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T08:49:31-07:00","id":1120832,"title":"","content":"So Arcanist, if this era wins, you're going to dump a whole bunch of books into the research thread I made for the Devs, right?<br /> <br /> I also think it's funny to note that even though this era was one of the ones I've been hounding them to do for Dark Eras 2, I voted fro Gilgamesh instead. That's how compelling this poll is. Geez, I hope the next one is a bit easier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T08:58:48-07:00","id":1120836,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120796#post1120796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Arcanist. I'm certainly not that cool.</div> </div> </div> </div>High praise coming from you, thank-you! (Oh my god Arc thinks I'm cool!)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Second Chances</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120832#post1120832\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Arcanist, if this era wins, you're going to dump a whole bunch of books into the research thread I made for the Devs, right?<br /> <br /> I also think it's funny to note that even though this era was one of the ones I've been hounding them to do for Dark Eras 2, I voted fro Gilgamesh instead. That's how compelling this poll is. Geez, I hope the next one is a bit easier.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also voted for Gilgamesh, actually. And I absolutely would! There's a whole lot of crap out there with people trying to shoehorn a suitable historic figure with as many tenuous connections of aspects of later legends into a roughly Arthur-shaped hole, so a tidy list of books that actually make anything resembling <i>sense </i>would probably be helpful.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 07-26-2017, 12:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T09:09:17-07:00","id":1120843,"title":"","content":"Someone higher in history-fu can answer on my theory? Mister <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4152\" data-vbnamecard=\"4152\">Arcanist</a>?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120774#post1120774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wait, so till 590s we do not have anything resembling Romance Arthur? Maybe it's like Merlin, as Silver Ladder Acanthus gone to one Roman commander in 450-480s and said to him: 'Hey, let's make this great kingdom, with your armies and my magic' - and then he let Arthur be killed and kingdom destroyed so he would Ascended? And then, as story become Supernal Truth, it REPEATED itself at least dozen times, with each new commander adding 'I must build my own kingdom!' And with passing years there is added this whole Roman Diamond 'we are Dragons' thing - and we end with 'Arthur is Pendragon, born/blessed by dragons' in end of early Middle Ages.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 07-26-2017, 12:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T09:23:51-07:00","id":1120852,"title":"","content":"Actually, we don't have anything even resembling Romance Arthur until the 1130s when Geoffrey of Monmouth does his History of the Kings of Britain. Before that it's oblique references in historical chronicles by both Welsh and Anglo-Saxon sources (mostly in the Welsh because the Chroniclers like to record solely victories). There's also a whole body of Welsh folklore written down where Arthur and his companions loom large, but don't <i>directly </i>feature in the majority.<br /> <br /> From the 6th Century until the 12th Century, what you mostly get isn't King Arthur, it's Arthur the Warlord, Arthur the Red Ravager of Britain, who once held a <i>hill </i>for three days under Saxon assault and then broke a shield wall <i>from the front with a cavalry charge</i>. (This is normally a spectacularly bad idea. Horses and spears don't go together.) Arthur, the man who it is said slew 960 warriors by his hand alone and smashed the Saxons so badly it was almost two generations before they could even raise a proper organized campaign again.<br /> <br /> EDIT: That said? There was a flood of people from the 6th through to the 7th centuries named something rather like Arthur all attempting to fill the shoes of a man who might not have even existed. Invariably, they all fail. They are, briefly, brilliant warriors who then fail in a spectacular manner, more or less dooming their kingdoms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 07-26-2017, 12:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T10:12:08-07:00","id":1120877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120843#post1120843\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Someone higher in history-fu can answer on my theory? Mister <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4152\" data-vbnamecard=\"4152\">Arcanist</a>?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Per Arcanist's comment, I think a better way to look at it is that Merlin hit the ascension gong so hard all of Britain rang with it for the next 200 years. It was still ringing by the time the continent caught up and incorporated what their ideals of the perfect, chivalric king and kingdom would be. But the note requires (or incorporates) doom as well as glory, so the legend always, always, always shows how they fail.<br /> <br /> In Arthur's case, by the way, that failure was not &quot;getting into a love triangle with my faithless wife&quot; but &quot;murdering a boatload of infants early on because I got afraid of prophecy&quot;. There are several takes on the legend that have him cursed as the &quot;Future King&quot; because he didn't get it right, then. Even in the literature--especially the compendium by Geoffrey--shows a thread of this, alongside Arthur's desperation to keep the party going.<br /> <br /> For this reason (as well as personal interest) I'd like to see the DE mix be Mage/Vampire, even as the setting would fit for several others. Vampire's dark tragedy and the &quot;vampires ruin everything&quot; meme fit well.<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T10:21:07-07:00","id":1120882,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khanwulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120877#post1120877\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For this reason (as well as personal interest) I'd like to see the DE mix be Mage/Vampire, even as the setting would fit for several others. Vampire's dark tragedy and the &quot;vampires ruin everything&quot; meme fit well.<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf</div> </div> </div> </div>Gonna be honest, I've been thinking about it and that's seemed to be a much better fit than Mage/Changeling. Mordred, the Bron and Grail, straight up Arthur, there's a lot you can do with vampires at that location and period.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T10:27:21-07:00","id":1120887,"title":"","content":"Plus, if we'll get Vampire for Arthur, we may get some new information about the Weihan Cynn, as if I remember correctly, their beginning was during the time Rome's control over Britain has collapsed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T10:42:30-07:00","id":1120892,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar886_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"886\" data-vbnamecard=\"886\">nofather</a> there's also the fact we've had werewolves portrayed only 400-ish years previously in Forsaken by Rome, and many of the themes for Britain will be similar--far from exact, but similar. For Vampire 4-500 is a dead zone with at best an aftertaste in the Fall of the Camarilla book. <br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120887#post1120887\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus, if we'll get Vampire for Arthur, we may get some new information about the Weihan Cynn, as if I remember correctly, their beginning was during the time Rome's control over Britain has collapsed.</div> </div> </div> </div>I rather thought the Weihan Cynn was associated with British Druidism and was therefore nearly stamped out by the Romans early on. Nearly, but not entirely.<br /> <br /> I'd really like to see more info on how Onyx Path views the fallout of the destruction of the Senex from the perspective of far-flung colonies. The population of Britain at that time could certainly sustain a good number of vampires, though the invasions would put huge pressure on feeding sources even as they bring new/other Germanic and Hunnic vamps....<br /> <br /> Lots to work with.<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T10:43:23-07:00","id":1120893,"title":"","content":"And as previously mentioned, there is a bloodline that already relates to grail lore. That said, If they actually have picked out eras for this vote already and just aren't telling us what they are so we vote by era instead of by gameline, I'm betting this is Mage/Changeling (and by extension, Gilgamesh is Werewolf/Beast and Jazz Age is Vampire/Mummy). If that's true, Vampire would be a good candidate for an expansion, although it may have to fight off Demon first.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T10:57:09-07:00","id":1120903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khanwulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120892#post1120892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I rather thought the Weihan Cynn was associated with British Druidism and was therefore nearly stamped out by the Romans early on. Nearly, but not entirely.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I've meant the beginning of their &quot;modern&quot; version (at least, modern when compared to Requiem for Regina)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T12:00:26-07:00","id":1120936,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120903#post1120903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've meant the beginning of their &quot;modern&quot; version (at least, modern when compared to Requiem for Regina)</div> </div> </div> </div>Aha, yes in that case. There would be a resurgence immediately following the collapse of Roman influence (i.e. Senex influence) that would pull together the pieces into what Regina represents. What puzzles me is, if the Weihan Cynn (this is a <i>Saxon</i> word formulation by the way) is pact-based magic at best and trading in favors at worst, where is Cruac at this time? Scattered in mystery cults? Hidden in Ireland with the Weihan Cynn's rivals?<br /> <br /> And the Legio Mortuum? They're... still around or just transitioning into the network later known as the Gallow's Post? (Or, both?)<br /> <br /> Thematically I can see Changeling being the second splat, but at the same time the Fae make use of such legends as the time creates, so do they really need to be integrally involved in the creation? I don't know.... Regardless, a treatment of the period will enable reasoned discussion and perhaps ancillary writing around what the other splats are doing. If any DE called for a more thorough take than just two, I'd say Arthurian Britain is it!<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T12:17:48-07:00","id":1120943,"title":"","content":"If we combine Arthurian age with Babylon do we get a Fate/CofD hack?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Historian ~ <b><a href=\"http://www.nwodbrasil.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">www.cronistasdastrevasbr.com</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>I currently ST a... </b>MtAW 2e campaign called &quot;Axis Mundi - Si Vis Pacem&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/250-yuukale","name":"Yuukale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T12:36:50-07:00","id":1120962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khanwulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120936#post1120936\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And the Legio Mortuum? They're... still around or just transitioning into the network later known as the Gallow's Post? (Or, both?)</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we would see the Legion of the Dead described in the Broken Covenants section of Vampire Second Edition, the Grim Battalion. That would be really cool to see as well. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/196-max-deltree","name":"Max Deltree","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"178"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T13:03:35-07:00","id":1120981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Max Deltree</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120962#post1120962\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think we would see the Legion of the Dead described in the Broken Covenants section of Vampire Second Edition, the Grim Battalion. That would be really cool to see as well. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It might be too soon after the collapse of the Senex, but it would be interesting to show how the Legion of the Dead is in the process of transitioning from a formal institution into a pack of unholy mercenaries backed up by a network of waystops and safe passage routes. Splitting, in other words, into the future Grim Battalion and Gallow's Post.<br /> <br /> However, Britain would be a fantastic place to insert something about the network of ancient, subterranean tunnels seemingly <i>gnawed</i> out of the stone, that run from Scotland all the way to Anatolia. They date back to prehistoric times and are not all seemlessly connected--that <i>we</i> can <i>tell</i>. A few devotions later and you'd be able to pass thin stone partitions and navigate in utter darkness like a pro. Or, if you will, like a Nospheratu Gallow's Post precursor.... <br /> <br /> --Khanwulf<br /> <br /> PS. Them tunnels? Totally real.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T15:08:53-07:00","id":1121042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khanwulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1120981#post1120981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">PS. Them tunnels? Totally real.</div> </div> </div> </div>The tunnels are real, but the idea that they're a network is fairly obviously wrong, it's nowhere close. Also, the nearest they come to Turkey seems to be Austria. Far as I can tell, the only people claiming a pan-European network of tunnels are the Daily Mail and Infowars.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-26T19:01:35-07:00","id":1121145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1121042#post1121042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The tunnels are real, but the idea that they're a network is fairly obviously wrong, it's nowhere close. Also, the nearest they come to Turkey seems to be Austria. Far as I can tell, the only people claiming a pan-European network of tunnels are the Daily Mail and Infowars.</div> </div> </div> </div>Turkey does have some ancient underground cities, however!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T09:34:49-07:00","id":1121345,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1121042#post1121042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The tunnels are real, but the idea that they're a network is fairly obviously wrong, it's nowhere close. Also, the nearest they come to Turkey seems to be Austria. Far as I can tell, the only people claiming a pan-European network of tunnels are the Daily Mail and Infowars.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Shhhh! World of Darkness! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T10:02:31-07:00","id":1121355,"title":"","content":"Alright, so, here is where things get <i>really </i>blurry. Because of course that isn't the end of it.<br /> <br /> <b>Fifth Century Britain Part Three: Arthur Roulette</b><br /> <br /> So Ambrosius makes it back to Britain just in time for the Saxon kingdoms to start pushing back into British territory. More Saxons are flooding into Britain because Clovis I of the Franks, one of the first kings of the Merovingians (oh, hey, look, a canonical Proximus dynasty) is uniting the Franks and the Gauls and doing a spectacular job of it. Europe isn't looking like such a great place to colonize and/or adventure in at the moment. In the late 470s a new Saxon leader emerges, a fellow by the name of Aelle who <i>doesn't </i>go for the traditional overland approach from established Saxon territory, instead overwhelming the southernmost Saxon Shore forts from the sea. Aelle brings three ships of men (as many as Hengest and Horsa before him) but unlike Hengest and Horsa, he doesn't try to take on all of Britain with them, just Sussex, and he has his three adult sons with him to back him up. He entrenches himself so well into Sussex that the British have to write the territory off as a lost cause. Aelle later becomes the first Saxon High King.<br /> <br /> With fewer soldiers on his side, more soldiers on the enemy side, and enemy warriors who have gotten used to his tactics, Ambrosius' second campaign is significantly less successful. There are still victories, but they are significantly less enduring. Aelle is constantly testing at his borders, and in 485 he breaks whatever peace he had with the surrounding British, having gained the backing of the other Saxon leaders on the south side of Britain, and communicating with the Angles (possibly Jutes, if that's where Hengest and Horsa's original land grant was, before Kent) in the north around Norfolk. The Anglo-Saxon groups, wherever they may be found (except for a group settled around Gloucester who may have been the ones that Vortigern initially settled in the 420s, and who revolted with Hengest and Horsa in the 440s) go on the warpath. This single allied group, the Gewisse, may have eventually formed the kernel around which Wessex later formed. This Saxon offensive all along the Southern and Eastern fronts was potentially met by two sustained campaigns from the British. Ambrosius, supported by the sons and grandsons of Vortigern, in the south, and an unknown leader based in the north who went all along the eastern front, potentially even fighting off resurgent Pict and Gaelic invasions from the north and the west.<br /> <br /> Ambrosius' lines started crumbling with a massive Saxon push in 491, starting with Aelle besieging the Saxon Shore fort on his northwestern border. The siege is a massacre where the Saxon Shore fort falls with &quot;not even one Briton left there&quot;. Saxons and Jutes moved northwest from their territory, Angles moved southwest from their lands, marching across central Britain and moving to break the unified ring of British kingdoms. Traditionally in 495, the first king of Wessex, Cerdic, arrives in Britain with his warriors, hemming in the British of the southern kingdoms. The Anglo-Saxons know they have to divide the British kingdoms to end the war, so they put everything they have into one assault. The unified British armies make a stand at an unknown location known only as Badon Hill. The Saxons lose, incredibly badly. So badly, in fact, that the majority of the Saxon kingdoms cannot raise an organized campaign for almost another forty or fifty years.<br /> <br /> Gildas, the only near-contemporary writer, ascribes the victory at Badon Hill to Ambrosius Aurelianus. Aelle's position and his aggressive action in the 480s and 490s fits with the traditional timeline given for the &quot;Arthur&quot; campaign, and the archaeology best supports a concentrated, unified Southern Britain, but doesn't account for more northern Anglo-Saxon/British conflict. The archaeology <i>also </i>supports tense Anglo-Saxon/British interactions around the Humber and in the area that would eventually become Bernicia.<br /> <br /> So, who the hell was Arthur, the man fighting this campaign leading up to the confrontation at Badon Hill? Let's look at out options:<ul><li>Ambrosius Aurelianus (~430s-500): Ambrosius definitely lead the second campaign against Hengest and Horsa in the late 450s into the 460s, considering Vortimer was dead and so was Vortigern is he wasn't so disgraced that he wouldn't even consider making a play for the High Kingship again. However, Ambrosius would be in his late fifties or early sixties by the time of Badon, which makes him unlikely to be the man leading the charge, and is even <i>less </i>likely to have lived to fight at Camlann. He must have delegated to <i>someone</i>.</li> <li>Riothamus (unknown-468): If he is a separate person from Ambrosius, there's still the problem of time period, the fact that we never hear from him again after Euric and the Visigoths slaughter his army (even though records say he survives) and there's no mention of him in Britain. He would also have needed to be able to lead at Badon, and live to die in combat around 515 CE at the legendary battle of Camlann.</li> <li>Pascent (~430s-500): The last surviving son of Vortigern, King of Powys, being the legendary Arthur? The timeline issue still crops up, but if Ambrosius was leading the military campaign against the Saxons he would need someone handling governance, someone he could trust. Nennius says that Ambrosius made Pascent governor of the &quot;province&quot; his father had ruled. While he almost certainly wasn't Arthur, he could probably be called Governor of Britain under High King (or Vicarius) Ambrosius.</li> <li>Cadell &quot;Bright-Hilt&quot; and Riocatus (~460s-530): Cadell was the son of Pascent, who might have been a penniless lord due to the disgrace of Vortiger, barring one exception. Virconium, formerly Vortigern's capital and the last thriving Roman city in Britain (the others were inhabited, but had nowhere near the same level of inhabitation or industry). A king known as &quot;Bright-Hilt&quot; ruling from the last thriving Romano-British city near several potential sites for Badon is quite compelling. His cousin Riocatus (King of Battle) appears to have been a cleric who eventually went to visit their uncle Faustus in Gaul, but returned around the time of Badon and may have even inherited the throne from Cadell.</li> <li>Owain White-Tooth and Cungelasus (450-530 and 480-560): There is literally no evidence that Owain was Arthur other than that he lived at the right time, ruled in Rhos, a sub-kingdom of Gwynedd, and was associated with a place known as &quot;Fortress of the Bear&quot;. His son, Cuneglasus/Cynlas the Red, was a brutal despot, who was famously known for divorcing his wife to marry her sister, a nun, after abducting his sister-in-law from the convent where she was living, but is outright called &quot;Bear&quot; (Arth) by Gildas. Gildas also ascribes to him &quot;arms unique to himself.&quot; He was also related to Maelgwn, King of Gwynedd, first attempted High King after Ambrosius and given the title &quot;Dragon of the Island&quot; by Gildas.</li> <li>Brychan and Dyfnwal (~430-500 and ~455-525): Two northern British kings (father and son) who were actually given the title Pendragon, but existing legends distinguishes between Arthur and Brychan, who in legend had to be stopped by Arthur and his warriors from rampaging across Britain and slaughtering a prince of Gwent who kidnapped his daughter. Most of his children entered the church as priests, monks, and nuns, including a son named Arthen.</li> <li>Agricola and Vortipor (~440-510 and ~470-540): Agricola is praised by Gildas as a good king, but his son Vortipor, called &quot;Protector&quot;, was one of the despots criticized by Gildas. Vortipor, a title like Vortigern or Vortimer, is written as &quot;Garthuir&quot; in Gaelic and he could have helped his father, who was an important Welsh king in the defence of Southern Britain, win at Badon Hill. He'd fit the classification of a high-ranking general who was not, himself, a king.</li> <li>Urien of Rheged (535-591): Too late to be Arthur, and a separate Arthurian character in his own right as Uriens of Gore, Urien king of Rheged was known for being one of the most fearsome warrior kings of the north with a famed band of cavalry. His brother, Lleuddun (Loth), his son Owain, and his nephew Gwrfan (possibly Gawain) all contributed to Arthurian lore. Urien's distant relative Morcant (Mordred?) was part of an alliance of five kings successfully keeping the Angles contained, but Morcant had Urien assassinated, and the alliance collapsed in the wake of his death, leaving the Angles free to act. This was also the period of activity for the bards Myrddin and Taliesin.</li> <li>Arthwys ap Mor (~450-520): The only man on this list whose name has the &quot;Art&quot; prefix, and King of Elmet in the north. There's a long literary tradition of a Northern Arthur, starting with his very first appearances in the written record. He is a descendent of Coel Hen (Old King Cole) who if he wasn't the last official Dux Britannium he almost definitely took over the position and kept passing that duty off to his relatives or allies, the warrior-kings known as the Men of the North. He was based out of York (Ebrauc) a Roman fortress-city, and his descendent's kingdoms were mostly situated around there. Arthwys may have given his name to the forest of Arthuret, where Myrddin would run mad almost half a century after his death. Arthwys' brother or son was El<b>uther</b>ius of the Great Host, whose sons were Peredur and Gwirgi (Percival for sure), known for possessing a warband of foot soldiers considered invincible when paired with the warriors (especially horsemen) of the other Men of the North. Elutherius is inaccurately attributed a son, Arthur &quot;Wing-Headed&quot; or &quot;Highest-Head&quot;, when he fact he had a daughter whose name was superficially similar to Arthur.</li> </ul>And that's the last of the fifth century. It's been a wild ride! Hope you enjoyed it. My personal pick for the &quot;core&quot; Arthur is Arthwys ap Mor, but a lot of men on this list, most notably Ambrosius Aurelianus, Cadell &quot;Bright-Hilt&quot;, and Urien of Rheged, and their relatives and descendants, all contributed to the legends that eventually became the Arthurian Romances. I'm going to be tinkering with my resources, seeing if I can update what I know and hammer out a reasonable, legible timeline. You know, with dates and stuff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 07-27-2017, 01:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T10:14:57-07:00","id":1121365,"title":"","content":"All but the shallowest of tunnels are Obfuscated by the remains of the Senex that lives on as a vampire type Illuminati. Invictus just thinks they're the true inheritors of the Senex.<br /> <br /> Edit: And if you write illuminati in lower case, count the number of letters, and subract the number of dots in the word, what do you get? That's right! VII!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":11887,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","user":"Tessie","date":". 07-27-2017, 01:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T10:29:17-07:00","id":1121374,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Khanwulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1121345#post1121345\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Shhhh! World of Darkness! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>It actually got me thinking. The purpose of the tunnels isn't really clear, and there's a lot of them so they might not be a literal network, but they could easily be a network of havens. a long tunnel is as a pretty good place for a kindred to sleep.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All but the shallowest of tunnels are Obfuscated </div> </div> </div>You don't really need to go that far, there's a naturally occurring system of tunnels, the Underworld. The tunnels could easily have Avernian Gates.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-27T11:36:33-07:00","id":1121426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1120287-de2-arthurian-discussion?p=1121374#post1121374\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You don't really need to go that far, there's a naturally occurring system of tunnels, the Underworld. The tunnels could easily have Avernian Gates.</div> </div> </div> </div>So what you're saying is that this secret cult of vampires might have their base in the land of the dead where they might, for example, safely store powerful Kindred laying in Torpor away from their enemies (such as Strix)?","signature":"